course: dictionary
lesson: 10736
title: Words for noise and anger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Uproar is a noun, a word for a thing. It names a big, messy noise, the sort made when lots of people shout at once. Look at the clues around it. «The room was in uproar» tells you that everyone was shouting and nobody was in order.
explain: The word can also be about people being angry in public. «An uproar over the new rules» means many people are cross about them and are making their feelings loud. The clues are the word over and the idea of new rules, which people argue about.
explain: Here is the example sentence: «The stoats, already jumpy from his morning trick, heard the uproar; some threw down their guns and ran.» Jumpy and ran are clues that the uproar was loud and frightening. Swap in loud, confused noise and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Uproar means a lot of loud, confused noise and excitement from many people.
fact: An uproar can also be a public storm of anger or protest about something.
fact: Uproar is usually uncountable, so we say «the uproar» or «an uproar», not «two uproars».
fact: In «Alice», the courtroom and the Queen's croquet game both end in uproar.
fact: In «The Wind in the Willows», the battle in Toad Hall is an uproar.
exercise: What is an uproar? | A small piece of news | *A loud, confused noise from many people | A soft whisper | A quiet, peaceful pause
exercise: In «an uproar over the new rules», what are most people feeling? | Sleepy and bored | Hungry and tired | *Angry and protesting | Happy and calm
exercise: In «The room was in uproar», what was happening in the room? | Everyone was asleep | *Everyone was shouting and out of order | Everyone was eating lunch | Everyone was reading quietly
test: «The courtroom ended in uproar.» What does ended in uproar mean? | The judge went home quietly | The court closed for lunch | *Everyone shouted and got out of order | Everyone fell asleep
test: In «The uproar lasted an hour», what kind of word is uproar? | An adjective describing a day | *A noun naming a thing | A full stop | A verb meaning to shout
exam: Which sentence uses uproar with the right meaning? | The baby slept soundly in her cot, a happy uproar | *The crowd's chanting turned the stadium into an uproar | The cat sat quietly by the fire, an uproar | The sun rose slowly over the calm, grey sea
